## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are constructed by coordination of the inorganic (connectors) and organic (linkers) components, exhibiting tuneable surface chemistry, pore shape and size [1,2]. The fine-tuning and systematic designing can be achieved to control the interactions between the host (framework) and guest species (gases) [3,4]. MOFs display several advantages over conventional porous materials and adsorbents, which have the ability to construct arrays in one-, two- or three-dimensions depending on the type of ligand and metal centres [3]. The features of structural diversity and large surface area in the MOFs allow them to enable high-performance gas capturing and separation applications [5].
In recent times, MOFs have come to light for the detection of gases as they have the ability to retain an appropriate amount of the gas molecules on their surface for interaction, given their highly porous structure [6,7]. The high selectivity for target gas can be realised through size sieving (i.e. specific pore size to control gas flow) and host–guest interaction (i.e. type of ligands with functional groups for specific gases and metal or open-metal sites to attract surface-charged gas molecules) [8]. The gas molecule interaction with the MOFs can be transduced using mechanical (e.g. surface acoustic wave device, quartz crystal microbalance, microcantilevers etc.), optical (e.g. luminescence, interferometry, vapochromism etc.) [6], and electrical platforms (e.g. capacitance, work function, resistance etc.) [9]. Among all these platforms, electrical-based chemiresistive transduction has been mostly studied due to its simplicity, portability and low-cost.
On the other hand, mixed-metal MOF structures, with a minimum of two different metal ions in the metal nodes realised by partial metal replacement in the nodes, may lead to the manipulation of physicochemical properties [10–12]. For example, Kirchon et al. demonstrated improved catalytic performances of Mn (II)- substituted (Porous coordination network) PCN-250 compared to that of intrinsic samples through investigating isomorphic substitution of a wide range of transition-metals (e.g. Co, Ni, Mn, and Fe), possibly attributed to the lower binding energy which provide fast shifting of oxidation states [13]. Similarly, Saliba et al. incorporated Co (II) in the ZIF-8 MOF structure via reaction–diffusion, showing improved visible light absorption ability as compared to pristine ZIF-8 [14]. Mixed-metal MOF strategy enables precise tuning of the metal oxidation state and metal ratios can be realised to manipulate the vacancies, conductivity, energy position of metal states, charge separation and synergetic effect, which are all important for gas adsorption and desorption [12,15,16]. However, the systematic study of the mixed-metals (Co, Zn, Mn, Fe) in the metal nodes of MOFs for the chemiresistive toxic gas sensing has not been explored and the corresponding functions of bimetallic MOFs are challenging to predict.
In this work, mixed-metal PCN-250 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}.$ , M: Co, Zn, Mn, and Fe) MOFs are synthesised using a hydrothermal approach and the effect of partial metal-replacement on toxic gas sensing capabilities is investigated. The PCN-250 framework, which is constructed by the coordination of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}–\upmu_{3}–0\uptimes0$ cluster and tetratopic azobenzene linker, [17] is selected due to the water stable structure and open metal redox-active sites [18]. The change in the morphological, structural, surface properties upon metal-substitution is studied through the simulated and experimental data. These series of homo– and hetero-metallic PCN-250 with modified properties are evaluated for ppb-levelled ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas sensing, in which the performance parameters including sensitivity and selectivity are extracted.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
The chemicals and solvents were purchased from the commercial seller (Sigma-Aldrich and Strem Chemical) and used without further modifications. During the preparation process, sodium acetate trihydrate, $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -Azobenzene tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\langleH_{4}A B T C)}$ , iron nitrate nonahydrate, cobalt nitrate, manganese nitrate, zinc nitrate, ethanol, dimethylformamide (DMF) and acetic acid were used.
## 2.2. Preparation of cluster
The metal cluster $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ $\mathsf{i}_{2}\mathsf{M}\ (\upmu_{3}0,\upmu=$ $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{F}\mathsf{e}$ or Co or Mn or Zn) was prepared by the reported method. In summary, sodium acetate trihydrate $(0.31\mathrm{\mol})$ was dissolved in $70~\mathrm{ml}$ of water to make a homogenous solution A. Solution B was prepared by dissolving iron nitrate nonahydrate $(0.02~\mathrm{mol}),$ and the metal nitrate $(0.1~\mathrm{mol})$ in $70~\mathrm{ml}$ water. Solution B was filtered and stirred, followed by the addition of solution A to obtain the brown precipitates after filtration. The obtained powder was washed with water and ethanol and kept for drying in the air. For $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{Z n}$ , the volume is $70~\mathrm{ml}$ in total, and the reddish-brown precipitate was obtained overnight.
## 2.3. Preparation of PCN-250
The $10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{ABTC}$ ligand and $15~\mathrm{mg}$ metal cluster $(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}})$ were dissolved ultrasonically in $1~\mathrm{ml}$ acetic acid and $2~\mathrm{ml}$ of DMF mixture in a glass container. The prepared mixture was left at $140\mathrm{{c}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ in an oven. The obtained product was cooled down at room temperature, followed by filtration to collect precipitate. The obtained powder was washed several times using water and ethanol and dried in a Vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight.
## 2.4. Computational details
In the present investigation, geometries of various first-row transition metal substituted heterometallic PCN-250 MOF component $\mathrm{Fe(III)_{2}M(I I)(\upmu_{3}{-}O)(C H_{3}C O O)_{6}}$ (where, $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , Fe(II), Co(II), and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II}).$ secondary building units (SBUs) and their corresponding ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas adsorbed complexes were optimised using M06-L (meta-generalised gradient density functional) method along with the def2-TZVP basis set $\mathrm{(TZVP=}$ valence triple- $\cdot\zeta$ polarised) developed by the Ahlrichs group [17,19,20]. We adopted the cluster model approach, which is a widely accepted method and agreed well with the experimental binding energy values. The cluster model used in our present investigation was cut from the optimised unit cell of corresponding heterometallic PCN-250 structures, which contain inorganic metal oxide unit $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}-\upmu_{3}{-}0$ $(\boldsymbol{00C})_{6}\mathrm{\cdot}$ , here $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , Fe(II), ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ , and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ . The terminal portion of the model clusters $(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{M})$ were saturated with a methyl group and the corresponding cluster units contain a total of 46 atoms. During optimisation, to restrain MOFs nature, we fixed the methyl carbon atoms of the model cluster and the remaining atoms and allow the gas molecules to relax without any symmetry constrain. We consider the following spin multiplicity $(2\thinspace s+1)$ for the various divalent substituted $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ (16), $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}$ (15), $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ (14) and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z\mathsf{n}$ (11) complexes, respectively. The binding energies of the ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas molecule with each of the heterometallic PCN-250 components were calculated using the following energy expression.
$$
\Delta E=E_{c l u s t e r-g a s}-(E_{c l u s t e r}+E_{g a s})
$$
where, $\Delta\pmb{E}$ represent the binding energy of the studied system, Ecluster-gas is the total energy of the complexes, $E_{c l u s t e r}$ is the energy of the individual cluster unit and $E_{g a s}$ is the energies of the isolated gas molecules, respectively. BEs of the complexes were further corrected for basis set superposition error (BSSE) using the counterpoise (CP) procedure. All the density functional theory calculations were carried out using the Gaussian 16 suite of program [21].
## 2.5. Characterisation
The morphologies of the synthesised samples were studied by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Verios 460L) at $25~\mathrm{kV}$ and $30\mathrm{~kV}.$ . X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained at Bruker D4 Endeavour having a CuKa X-ray source with a wavelength of $1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to study the crystal structure. Perklin Elmer Spectrum 100 Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was used to study the spectra ranging between 600 and $4000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Micrometrics 3Flex Adsorption Analyser was utilised to measure the pore size distribution and surface area through $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ gas adsorption at 77 K. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and XPS valence data were obtained on Thermo Scientific K-Alpha XPS spectrometer having monochromatic Al $\operatorname{K}_{\mathfrak{X}}$ X-ray source $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}h v}=1486.7~\mathrm{eV}$ ). Agilent Cary 60 Spectrophotometer was employed to obtain the ultraviolet–visible (UV–Vis) absorption spectra. Further, Tauc plots of the samples from UV–Vis data were calculated to identify the optical bandgap. Asylum MFP-3D Infinity Atomic Force Microscope equipped with a Multi75E-G tip was utilised for the Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) measurements and images were analysed using Gwyddion v2.7 to calculate the contact pressure difference $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{CPD}})$ in order to measure the work function.
## 2.6. Gas sensing studies
Bimetallic MOFs was mixed with the $2\%$ carbon black (CB) in ethanol solution as reported previously [22]. In summary, CB and MOF were dispersed in two different ethanol solutions and mixed together, followed by further sonication to obtain a homogenous mixture. $2~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ink containing $14.3~\upmu\up g$ PCN-250 sample and $0.29~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ conductive carbon black powder was drop casted on the interdigital electrode (IDE) substrates to maintain the electrical conductivities of samples within the measurable range by the multimeter. Figure S1 displays the cross-sectional view of PCN-250 $(\mathrm{Fe_{2}M n})$ and PCN-250 $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\right)$ coatings and the thickness of the coated film for all PCN-250 samples. The thickness of different PCN-250 films coated with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}C_{0}$ , ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ are 16.01, 17.30, 17.78 and $21.52\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . LINKAM stage (Scientific Instruments, customised gas testing chamber) and multichannel flow controller (MKS 1479A, U.S.A) was utilised to conduct gas sensing experiments and control the different gases flow. The gas sensitivity is evaluated for the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas and selectivity is compared against various gases like $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at room temperature.
## 3. Results and discussion
The morphological properties of synthesised PCN-250 based homo- and hetero-metallic MOFs were studied using SEM. Fig. 1 a-d show that the morphologies of all hetero-metallic crystals are polyhedral in shape, indicating the maintenance of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ PCN-250 framework despite the partial substitution of foreign metal atoms [23]. However, while pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ PCN-250 is with an average size of $\sim4.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , the partial replacement of Fe in the framework with Co, Mn, and Zn results in the reduction of the particle size to $\sim0.5$ , ${\sim}0.7$ , and $0.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively, as also confirmed by the statistical analysis (Figure S2). The addition of divalent metals in the metal cluster causes a severe reduction of crystal size, attributed to the variation of the cationic atom size [24,25]. Further, the partial replacement of the trivalent metals in the MOF metal nodes with the divalent atoms may play a role in increasing the nucleation sites in the presence of heterometallic metal clusters, possibly resulting in the growth of smaller sized MOF crystals with similar morphologies [26].”
In addition, the XRD patterns reveal that all $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 samples share an identical crystal structure (Fig. 2a), indicating that the foreign metal substitution does not alter the MOF framework [13]. Further, the chemical bonding structures of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 were analysed using FTIR (Fig. 2b). The peaks range between 1675 and $650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are mainly attributed to the presence of carbon-based linkers in the structure (e.g. $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching, $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching, $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bending, $c{-}0$ stretching, $0{=}C-$ O stretching) [22]. The azobenezene signature peaks of $N=N$ are found around $1375~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1452~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [27,28]. The peak around $620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the presence of stretching of the Fe-O bond in both homo- and hetero-metallic MOFs [29]. The vibration at 716 and $532~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate the presence of Mn-O bond in the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ cluster [30]. The peaks at 650 and $535~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ show the stretching of Co-O bond in the $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ metal cluster [31]. The stretching frequency around 683 and $465~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to the presence of $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ in the case of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ cluster [32]. The metal-oxygen stretching vibrations present below $650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate the presence of bimetallic clusters of MOFs. The ${\sf N}_{2}$ gas adsorption at $77~\mathrm{K}$ is performed to obtain the adsorption isotherms. All samples display the type I isotherm (Fig. 2c), with comparable Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and porosity with respect to previously reported PCN-250 [13]. The measured surface areas of different isostructural PCN-250 of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}$ - Co, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ are 1782.1, 1456.9, 1276.8, and $1521.9\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , respectively and average pore diameter by BET are 16.7, 16.8, 16.8 and $16.5~\mathrm{~\AA},$ , respectively as shown in Table S1 and pore size distribution in Figure S3.
XPS measurements are used for revealing the chemical compositions and bonding states of the samples, in which the C1s peak at $284.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ is utilised as a reference for the peak shift correction. Fig. 3a displays the doublet peaks of Fe at $\sim711.6$ eV (Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{.}$ ) and $\sim725.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathrm{.}$ ) in the metal cluster of all PCN-250 samples, attributed to the Fe-O bond. In addition, the O1s peaks at $\sim531.8\mathrm{eV}$ indicate the presence of $\mathbf{M}{-}0$ cluster and $c{-}0$ bond (Fig. 3b), while the N1s peaks at $\sim400.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the azobenzene presence in the linker component of the MOF structure representing $N=N$ and $_{\mathsf{N-C}}$ bond (Fig. 3c) [33]. Upon the partial substitution of foreign metal atoms, $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ PCN-250 exhibits the $\mathtt{C o2p}$ spectrum with peaks at $\sim781.7\$ eV $(\mathsf C02\mathsf p_{3/2})$ ) and $\sim797.5$ $(\mathsf{C o}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ (Fig. 3d), both ascribed to the Co-O bond in the $C0^{2+}$ oxidation state. According to Fig. 3e, Mn 2p peaks in $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ PCN-250 are found at $\sim641.3\mathrm{eV}$ (Mn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ ) and $\sim653.8\mathrm{eV}$ $(\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}2\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}}_{3/2},$ ), indicating the presence of Mn-O bond in the $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ oxidation state. From Fig. 3f, $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ peaks at 1022.1 eV $(\mathsf{Z n~}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ ) and $1045.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(Z\mathsf{n}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ are attributed to the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in the metal-oxo cluster for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ PCN-250 [34–36].
To determine the electronic band structure of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250, bandgap energies, Fermi level positions $\left(\mathrm{E_{f}}\right)$ , and the valence band maxima (VBM) positions are investigated using the Tauc plot derived from the UV–Vis absorption spectra (Fig. 4a), KPFM (Figure S4), and XPS valence spectra (Fig. 4b), respectively. The bandgap differences between the conduction band maxima (CBM) and VBM are found to be 4.71, 4.64, 4.12 and $4.01\ \mathrm{{\eV}}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Co}$ , ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ PCN-250, respectively. The corresponding VBM positions are 1.7, 2.0, 1.8 and $1.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ away from the Fermi level, respectively, while the Fermi level positions are 5.54, 5.56, 5.61 and $5.50\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively. As a result, electronic band structures of four $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 samples are shown in Fig. 4c, indicating the commonly $p$ -type semiconducting feature. Both positions of VBM and CBM are shifted after the substitution of foreign metal atoms in the host metal nodes, possibly due to the hybridisation with the existing orbitals [37,38]. The expansion of bandgap energies of hetero-metallic PCN-250 crystals may also be attributed to the size reduction effect confirmed by SEM imaging in Fig. 1 [39–41].
The $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas sensing capabilities of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 samples are assessed using both the computational and experimental approaches. First of all, the M06-L/def2-TZVP (meta-generalised gradient density functional/valence triple zeta polarization) level of theory is used for revealing the interactional behaviour of the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas with the PCN-250 homo- and hetero-metallic MOFs at different possible orientations of the complexes (Table S2). The bond lengths are determined given optimised geometries of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas molecules with the exposed metal sites of metallic clusters, which are ranged between 2.01 and $2.34\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Fig. 5). Furthermore, for maximum possible binding energies, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 (M: Mn or Co) divalent-metal atom interacts with the N atom of the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas while the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 (M: Fe or $Z\mathrm{n}$ ) interacts with one of the oxygen atoms of the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas. The calculated binding energies between metal centres of different MOFs with $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ show the following trend of $\mathrm{Fe_{2}M n}>\mathrm{Fe_{2}C o}>\mathrm{Fe_{3}}>\mathrm{Fe_{2}Z n}_{\mathrm{t}}$ , suggesting ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ with the largest interaction capability with the ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas. Furthermore, it is expected that ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ PCN-250 induces stronger charge transfer with the adsorbed ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas molecules owing to the lower electronegative value and larger ionic radius of Mn compared to those of Co, Zn, and Fe. The calculated binding energies between metal centres of the ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ PCN-250 sample and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ gases are lower than that of ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ , revealing the unique selectivity towards $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas which are similar to the experimental investigation results shown in Fig. 6c. Such a unique selectivity is also observed in other heterometallic PCN-250 samples, further confirming that their gas sensing performances are governed by surface adsorption energies. A slight discrepancy is found in pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ PCN-250 as the response magnitude of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ is smaller compared to that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , possibly ascribed to the rapid diffusion of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ gas across the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ MOF framework.
The experimental $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas sensing performances of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{M}$ PCN-250 samples were investigated through the chemiresistive transducing platform at room temperature. The response factor of a gas sensor is calculated by the ratio of the variation of resistance $\left(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{target{\mathrm{~\gas~}}}}-\mathrm{~R}_{\mathrm{o}}\right)$ to the resistance of baseline $(\mathsf{R}_{0})$ . From Fig. 6a, the response factors of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ PCN-250 turn to negative upon the exposure of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas with different concentrations between 60 and 500 ppb, confirming its $p$ -type semiconducting feature as shown in Fig. 4c. Upon the exposure of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas, the adsorption of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas molecules on the surface of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}$ - Mn induces the charge transfer, extracting the electrons from the material body to the gas-matter interface to form into dipoles. Therefore, the hole concentration in the $p$ -type MOF is increased, improving the overall electrical conductivity. The highest response factor ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ PCN-250 is achieved at $2.7\%$ for 500 ppb $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas, which is improved over those of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ $(1.3\%)$ $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ $(0.7\%)$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ $(0.6\%)$ (Fig. 6b). Such an experimental trend matches well with the computational studies, in which ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{Mn}}$ demonstrates the largest adsorption binding energy towards $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas (Fig. 5). The gas selectivity results in Fig. 6c indicates that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}\mathrm{PCN}{-250}$ is highly selective towards the $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ with a ppb-levelled concentration, in which the response magnitudes for $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ $(1\%)$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(10\%)$ , and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $(10\%)$ are only $1.5\%$ , $1.4\%$ , and $0.4\%$ , respectively. In comparison, both pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ and substituted $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ PCN-250 display relatively poor selectivity given the comparable response magnitudes between $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
In comparison, ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ gas sensing performances of most MOFs are usually investigated at elevated temperatures of $>200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Table 1), while irreversible response occurs at lower temperatures below $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (e.g. for the case of Ni-MOF-74 [42]. As a result, reversible room-temperature-operated MOF-based $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas sensors are rarely found, in which $\mathsf{H}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{M o O}_{3}.$ /FeMOF composite has been the representative so far (response magnitude of $2.1\%$ at $1\mathsf{p p m N O}_{2}$ response/ recovery time of 6000/6000 s) [22]. Our heterometallic $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ MOF demonstrates enhanced $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ sensing performances in terms of response magnitude and kinetics compared to that of $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{MoO}_{3}/$ FeMOF composite.
## 4. Conclusion
We successfully synthesised PCN-250 $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{M}^{\mathrm{II}})$ MOFs with stable crystal structures and open-metal sites from four different variants of M including Co, Mn, Zn, and Fe through a hydrothermal approach, in which the effect of metal substitution on the toxic $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas sensing was investigated at room temperature. In reference to the pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ PCN-250 MOF, the partial replacement of Fe in PCN-250 resulted in the size reduction to the sub- $\cdot\upmu\mathrm{m}$ scale, while the MOF framework was maintained. In addition, both positions of CBM and VBM were altered, leading to a slight increase in bandgap energies. More importantly, the binding energies of $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas molecules on the MOF surface were manipulated, in which the hetero-metallic $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ exhibited the largest value through the computational investigation. Similarly, experimental data showed that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ PCN-250 demonstrated a typical $p$ -type sensor response with a $\sim208\%$ , $\sim386\%$ and $\sim450\%$ larger magnitude for 500 ppb ${\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ compared to pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ , $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{C o}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}Z\mathrm{n}$ , respectively. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}$ PCN-250 exhibited a high selectivity of ppblevelled $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ gas over commonly used gases in the industry (e.g. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ). This work provides a feasible approach to engineer the surface adsorption and electronic band structure properties of MOFs, leading to the development of flexible candidate materials for high-performance gas capture and sensing devices.
